{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DMoQaAQHjeNUHnnTG6cB5E","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t//! TLS-for-QUIC trait surface, modeled on `quinn-proto::crypto`.\n2\t//!\n3\t//! Quinn dependency: **none** — we lift the trait shapes\n4\t//! verbatim into our own module.  Borrow inspiration; don't\n5\t//! link the crate.\n6\t//!\n7\t//! This module defines the trait surface; concrete backends\n8\t//! (picotls, rustls, OpenSSL) live in sibling modules\n9\t//! (`tls_picotls.rs`, `tls_rustls.rs`, `tls_openssl.rs`)\n10\t//! gated by the matching Cargo features.\n11\t//!\n12\t//! Phase 4 keeps this module as the TLS trait contract; concrete\n13\t//! backend behavior lives in sibling modules.\n14\t//!\n15\t//! ## Structure\n16\t//!\n17\t//! * [`Session`] — per-connection TLS state.  One impl per\n18\t//!   backend; each backend wraps its native session type.\n19\t//!   Replaces the C `*mut c_void` `tls_ctx` and `tls_sendbuf`\n20\t//!   fields.\n21\t//!\n22\t//! * [`ClientConfig`] / [`ServerConfig`] — builder traits;\n23\t//!   pick one to start a [`Session`].\n24\t//!\n25\t//! * [`PacketKey`] — packet protection (the AEAD layer).\n26\t//!   Hides the underlying AEAD primitive from picoquic.\n27\t//!\n28\t//! * [`HeaderKey`] — header protection.  See also\n29\t//!   [`crate::header_protection::HeaderProtector`], which is the\n30\t//!   concrete implementation; backends supply `HeaderKey`s\n31\t//!   that delegate to it.\n32\t//!\n33\t//! * [`Keys`] / [`KeyPair`] — bundles of keys per epoch.\n34\t//!\n35\t//! * [`TlsCallbacks`] — application-supplied hooks the TLS\n36\t//!   layer invokes (ALPN selection, ticket store, certificate\n37\t//!   verification).  One trait, default impls everywhere\n38\t//!   except the genuinely application-specific bits.\n39\t\n40\textern crate alloc;\n41\tuse alloc::boxed::Box;\n42\tuse alloc::vec::Vec;\n43\t\n44\tuse crate::Error;\n45\t\n46\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n47\t// Per-connection TLS state.\n48\t\n49\t/// Per-connection TLS state.  Drives the handshake forward,\n50\t/// surfaces handshake events, exposes negotiated keys.\n51\t///\n52\t/// Modeled on `quinn_proto::crypto::Session`.  The `Send` bound\n53\t/// is preserved so a connection can be moved between threads\n54\t/// even though picoquic itself is single-threaded in v1\n55\t/// (matters for v2 multi-threading and for users who run\n56\t/// multiple Quic contexts on different threads).\n57\tpub trait Session: Send {\n58\t    /// Read handshake bytes received from the peer.  Returns\n59\t    /// `Ok(true)` when the handshake transitions to a new\n60\t    /// epoch (new keys are now available via\n61\t    /// [`Self::next_1rtt_keys`] / [`Self::write_handshake`]).\n62\t    fn read_handshake(&mut self, plaintext: &[u8]) -> Result<bool, Error>;\n63\t\n64\t    /// Write handshake bytes for the peer.  Returns\n65\t    /// `Some(Keys)` when an epoch transition produces fresh\n66\t    /// outbound keys.\n67\t    fn write_handshake(&mut self, buf: &mut Vec<u8>) -> Option<Keys>;\n68\t\n69\t    /// `true` until the handshake completes.  After the\n70\t    /// handshake the session is in steady state and this\n71\t    /// returns `false`.\n72\t    fn is_handshaking(&self) -> bool;\n73\t\n74\t    /// Negotiated 1-RTT keys.  Returns `Some` once after the\n75\t    /// handshake completes; subsequent calls return `None`\n76\t    /// until a key update.\n77\t    fn next_1rtt_keys(&mut self) -> Option<KeyPair>;\n78\t\n79\t    /// Server-side handshake parameters (SNI, ALPN, etc.).\n80\t    /// Available after the first ClientHello.\n81\t    fn handshake_data(&self) -> Option<HandshakeData>;\n82\t\n83\t    /// Peer's certificate chain (or whatever identity material\n84\t    /// the backend exposes).  Available after the handshake.\n85\t    fn peer_identity(&self) -> Option<PeerIdentity>;\n86\t\n87\t    /// Early-data (0-RTT) keys, if the session offers them.\n88\t    fn early_keys(&self) -> Option<(Box<dyn HeaderKey>, Box<dyn PacketKey>)>;\n89\t\n90\t    /// Whether the server accepted the client's 0-RTT data.\n91\t    fn early_data_accepted(&self) -> Option<bool>;\n92\t\n93\t    /// Decode the peer's QUIC transport parameters (TLS\n94\t    /// extension 57; RFC 9001 §8.2).  Returns the decoded\n95\t    /// transport parameters as raw bytes — picoquic parses\n96\t    /// them with its own decoder.\n97\t    fn transport_parameters(&self) -> Result<Option<Vec<u8>>, Error>;\n98\t\n99\t    /// HKDF-Expand-Label using the session's exporter master\n100\t    /// secret.  Used by QUIC for stateless-reset tokens etc."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1a5c7007-b115-47e8-a801-5bdd15835036","uuid":"43cd75fa-0658-4ae0-b142-782d1e795165","timestamp":"2026-05-07T06:49:52.656Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/richbarn/Projects/picoquic/rs/fq/src/tls.rs","content":"//! TLS-for-QUIC trait surface, modeled on `quinn-proto::crypto`.\n//!\n//! Quinn dependency: **none** — we lift the trait shapes\n//! verbatim into our own module.  Borrow inspiration; don't\n//! link the crate.\n//!\n//! This module defines the trait surface; concrete backends\n//! (picotls, rustls, OpenSSL) live in sibling modules\n//! (`tls_picotls.rs`, `tls_rustls.rs`, `tls_openssl.rs`)\n//! gated by the matching Cargo features.\n//!\n//! Phase 4 keeps this module as the TLS trait contract; concrete\n//! backend behavior lives in sibling modules.\n//!\n//! ## Structure\n//!\n//! * [`Session`] — per-connection TLS state.  One impl per\n//!   backend; each backend wraps its native session type.\n//!   Replaces the C `*mut c_void` `tls_ctx` and `tls_sendbuf`\n//!   fields.\n//!\n//! * [`ClientConfig`] / [`ServerConfig`] — builder traits;\n//!   pick one to start a [`Session`].\n//!\n//! * [`PacketKey`] — packet protection (the AEAD layer).\n//!   Hides the underlying AEAD primitive from picoquic.\n//!\n//! * [`HeaderKey`] — header protection.  See also\n//!   [`crate::header_protection::HeaderProtector`], which is the\n//!   concrete implementation; backends supply `HeaderKey`s\n//!   that delegate to it.\n//!\n//! * [`Keys`] / [`KeyPair`] — bundles of keys per epoch.\n//!\n//! * [`TlsCallbacks`] — application-supplied hooks the TLS\n//!   layer invokes (ALPN selection, ticket store, certificate\n//!   verification).  One trait, default impls everywhere\n//!   except the genuinely application-specific bits.\n\nextern crate alloc;\nuse alloc::boxed::Box;\nuse alloc::vec::Vec;\n\nuse crate::Error;\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Per-connection TLS state.\n\n/// Per-connection TLS state.  Drives the handshake forward,\n/// surfaces handshake events, exposes negotiated keys.\n///\n/// Modeled on `quinn_proto::crypto::Session`.  The `Send` bound\n/// is preserved so a connection can be moved between threads\n/// even though picoquic itself is single-threaded in v1\n/// (matters for v2 multi-threading and for users who run\n/// multiple Quic contexts on different threads).\npub trait Session: Send {\n    /// Read handshake bytes received from the peer.  Returns\n    /// `Ok(true)` when the handshake transitions to a new\n    /// epoch (new keys are now available via\n    /// [`Self::next_1rtt_keys`] / [`Self::write_handshake`]).\n    fn read_handshake(&mut self, plaintext: &[u8]) -> Result<bool, Error>;\n\n    /// Write handshake bytes for the peer.  Returns\n    /// `Some(Keys)` when an epoch transition produces fresh\n    /// outbound keys.\n    fn write_handshake(&mut self, buf: &mut Vec<u8>) -> Option<Keys>;\n\n    /// `true` until the handshake completes.  After the\n    /// handshake the session is in steady state and this\n    /// returns `false`.\n    fn is_handshaking(&self) -> bool;\n\n    /// Negotiated 1-RTT keys.  Returns `Some` once after the\n    /// handshake completes; subsequent calls return `None`\n    /// until a key update.\n    fn next_1rtt_keys(&mut self) -> Option<KeyPair>;\n\n    /// Server-side handshake parameters (SNI, ALPN, etc.).\n    /// Available after the first ClientHello.\n    fn handshake_data(&self) -> Option<HandshakeData>;\n\n    /// Peer's certificate chain (or whatever identity material\n    /// the backend exposes).  Available after the handshake.\n    fn peer_identity(&self) -> Option<PeerIdentity>;\n\n    /// Early-data (0-RTT) keys, if the session offers them.\n    fn early_keys(&self) -> Option<(Box<dyn HeaderKey>, Box<dyn PacketKey>)>;\n\n    /// Whether the server accepted the client's 0-RTT data.\n    fn early_data_accepted(&self) -> Option<bool>;\n\n    /// Decode the peer's QUIC transport parameters (TLS\n    /// extension 57; RFC 9001 §8.2).  Returns the decoded\n    /// transport parameters as raw bytes — picoquic parses\n    /// them with its own decoder.\n    fn transport_parameters(&self) -> Result<Option<Vec<u8>>, Error>;\n\n    /// HKDF-Expand-Label using the session's exporter master\n    /// secret.  Used by QUIC for stateless-reset tokens etc.","numLines":100,"startLine":1,"totalLines":390}}}
