{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0144c7o75bvJ2pjxNT3oHK8H","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Implementation translation guide (Phase 4)\n2\t\n3\tA focused reference for filling in `todo!()` bodies in\n4\t`rs/fq/src/`.  Companion to `xlate/test_translation_guide.md`\n5\t(which covers the test side); they share the API surface\n6\toverview and naming conventions, but this guide focuses on\n7\t*body* translation.\n8\t\n9\t**Read this first.**\n10\t\n11\t---\n12\t\n13\t## NO SHORTCUTS — the prime directive\n14\t\n15\tPhase 4's job is to **translate the entire library**, not to fill\n16\tthe easy bodies and stub the hard ones.  Past sweeps have produced\n17\thundreds of \"completed\" stubs that returned `None` / `Err(Generic)`\n18\t/ `Ok(())` plus a `// SKIP:` comment.  **That is failure, not\n19\tcompletion.**  The gate now flags `todo!()`, `unimplemented!()`,\n20\tand `// SKIP:` markers all the same.\n21\t\n22\tIf a body is hard — borrow checker, lifetimes, missing API\n23\tsurface, missing struct fields — **do the hard work**:\n24\t\n25\t* If the borrow checker says `&mut` from `&self`, use the actual\n26\t  Rust idiom (`&mut self`, `RefCell`, restructure the call site).\n27\t  Don't return `None` and call it done.\n28\t* If a method needs a static table the C side has, **port the\n29\t  table** to a Rust `static` / `const`.  Don't fabricate a\n30\t  placeholder.\n31\t* If the Rust API is missing a field a C body uses, **add the\n32\t  field** to the Rust struct.  Don't skip the function.\n33\t* If the Rust API is missing a helper a C body needs, **translate\n34\t  the helper first**, then come back.\n35\t\n36\tThe only legitimate way to leave a body unfinished is `todo!()`\n37\twith a clear blocker note above it explaining what it depends on.\n38\t**Never `unimplemented!()`, never `// SKIP:`, never a fake stub\n39\treturn** — those hide the work behind the gate.\n40\t\n41\t---\n42\t\n43\t## Operating mandate\n44\t\n45\t* **Translate every C body faithfully.**  Walk `picoquic/<src>.c`\n46\t  for each function whose Rust counterpart is incomplete and write\n47\t  the equivalent Rust body.  Don't sidestep with a simpler-looking\n48\t  algorithm — the C body is the spec.\n49\t* **Tests are the gate.**  After each meaningful chunk, run\n50\t  `cd rs/fq && cargo test` and watch the panic count drop.  A\n51\t  function is \"done\" when the tests that reach it stop panicking.\n52\t* **Idiomatic Rust over C-mirroring.**  Phase 1 already shaped\n53\t  signatures.  Phase 4 is where the shape pays off — use Rust\n54\t  idioms in the body even when they don't match the C control\n55\t  flow.\n56\t* **No `unsafe`.**  No edits outside `rs/fq/src/`.\n57\t\n58\t---\n59\t\n60\t## C body patterns → Rust idioms\n61\t\n62\t### Memory and ownership\n63\t\n64\t| C pattern | Rust translation |\n65\t|---|---|\n66\t| `T* p = malloc(sizeof(T)); ...; free(p);` | `let p = Box::new(T { ... });` (auto `Drop`) |\n67\t| `T arr[N]` on stack, returns by ptr | `[T; N]` or `&[T; N]`, never `*const T` |\n68\t| `(uint8_t* buf, size_t len)` aliasing call | `&[u8]` (read) / `&mut [u8]` (write); slice carries len |\n69\t| Linked list: `T* head; T* cur->next` | `Vec<T>` or `VecDeque<T>` if FIFO; iterate with `.iter()` |\n70\t| Intrusive node: `struct T { …; next *T; }` | Token-based: parent owns `Vec<T>`, callers hold an [`Arena`]/[`SplayTree`]/[`HashTable`] token |\n71\t| `void* opaque_handle` | `Box<dyn Trait>` (one trait per shape) |\n72\t| `static T global` | `static T` if `T: Sync` else `pub fn name() -> T` accessor |\n73\t\n74\t### Control flow\n75\t\n76\t| C pattern | Rust translation |\n77\t|---|---|\n78\t| `if (ret == 0) { ... } else { ret = -1; }` | `if x { … }`; propagate fallibility with `?` |\n79\t| `goto cleanup; cleanup: free(p); return ret;` | Rust `Drop` runs automatically; `?` for early-return |\n80\t| `int ret = 0; ... ret \\|= f(...); ... return ret;` | Compose `Result` with `?`; never use bitwise-OR for status |"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1a5c7007-b115-47e8-a801-5bdd15835036","uuid":"3a005647-152b-4dab-9538-4bfef20c2f88","timestamp":"2026-05-07T06:49:53.032Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/richbarn/Projects/picoquic/xlate/impl_translation_guide.md","content":"# Implementation translation guide (Phase 4)\n\nA focused reference for filling in `todo!()` bodies in\n`rs/fq/src/`.  Companion to `xlate/test_translation_guide.md`\n(which covers the test side); they share the API surface\noverview and naming conventions, but this guide focuses on\n*body* translation.\n\n**Read this first.**\n\n---\n\n## NO SHORTCUTS — the prime directive\n\nPhase 4's job is to **translate the entire library**, not to fill\nthe easy bodies and stub the hard ones.  Past sweeps have produced\nhundreds of \"completed\" stubs that returned `None` / `Err(Generic)`\n/ `Ok(())` plus a `// SKIP:` comment.  **That is failure, not\ncompletion.**  The gate now flags `todo!()`, `unimplemented!()`,\nand `// SKIP:` markers all the same.\n\nIf a body is hard — borrow checker, lifetimes, missing API\nsurface, missing struct fields — **do the hard work**:\n\n* If the borrow checker says `&mut` from `&self`, use the actual\n  Rust idiom (`&mut self`, `RefCell`, restructure the call site).\n  Don't return `None` and call it done.\n* If a method needs a static table the C side has, **port the\n  table** to a Rust `static` / `const`.  Don't fabricate a\n  placeholder.\n* If the Rust API is missing a field a C body uses, **add the\n  field** to the Rust struct.  Don't skip the function.\n* If the Rust API is missing a helper a C body needs, **translate\n  the helper first**, then come back.\n\nThe only legitimate way to leave a body unfinished is `todo!()`\nwith a clear blocker note above it explaining what it depends on.\n**Never `unimplemented!()`, never `// SKIP:`, never a fake stub\nreturn** — those hide the work behind the gate.\n\n---\n\n## Operating mandate\n\n* **Translate every C body faithfully.**  Walk `picoquic/<src>.c`\n  for each function whose Rust counterpart is incomplete and write\n  the equivalent Rust body.  Don't sidestep with a simpler-looking\n  algorithm — the C body is the spec.\n* **Tests are the gate.**  After each meaningful chunk, run\n  `cd rs/fq && cargo test` and watch the panic count drop.  A\n  function is \"done\" when the tests that reach it stop panicking.\n* **Idiomatic Rust over C-mirroring.**  Phase 1 already shaped\n  signatures.  Phase 4 is where the shape pays off — use Rust\n  idioms in the body even when they don't match the C control\n  flow.\n* **No `unsafe`.**  No edits outside `rs/fq/src/`.\n\n---\n\n## C body patterns → Rust idioms\n\n### Memory and ownership\n\n| C pattern | Rust translation |\n|---|---|\n| `T* p = malloc(sizeof(T)); ...; free(p);` | `let p = Box::new(T { ... });` (auto `Drop`) |\n| `T arr[N]` on stack, returns by ptr | `[T; N]` or `&[T; N]`, never `*const T` |\n| `(uint8_t* buf, size_t len)` aliasing call | `&[u8]` (read) / `&mut [u8]` (write); slice carries len |\n| Linked list: `T* head; T* cur->next` | `Vec<T>` or `VecDeque<T>` if FIFO; iterate with `.iter()` |\n| Intrusive node: `struct T { …; next *T; }` | Token-based: parent owns `Vec<T>`, callers hold an [`Arena`]/[`SplayTree`]/[`HashTable`] token |\n| `void* opaque_handle` | `Box<dyn Trait>` (one trait per shape) |\n| `static T global` | `static T` if `T: Sync` else `pub fn name() -> T` accessor |\n\n### Control flow\n\n| C pattern | Rust translation |\n|---|---|\n| `if (ret == 0) { ... } else { ret = -1; }` | `if x { … }`; propagate fallibility with `?` |\n| `goto cleanup; cleanup: free(p); return ret;` | Rust `Drop` runs automatically; `?` for early-return |\n| `int ret = 0; ... ret \\|= f(...); ... return ret;` | Compose `Result` with `?`; never use bitwise-OR for status |","numLines":80,"startLine":1,"totalLines":339}}}
